<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Botorrita</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Botorrita is a village in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, on the site of the Celtiberian town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/contrebia-belaisca">Contrebia Belaisca</a>. Excavations there found four inscribed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronze-plaques">bronze plaques</a> of the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE, three in the Celtiberian language and one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> recording a water-rights dispute judged by a Roman governor in 87 BCE. The Celtiberian plaques are the longest known texts in that language.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/botorrita.jpg" alt="Botorrita" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vikyto · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cielo_07.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
